  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia funded the UNFPA to continue conducting the 15-year Longitudinal Cohort Study on the Filipino Child. This Study tracks the lives of 5,000 children to help policymakers address the developmental needs of Filipino youth and harness the country&#x2019;s demographic dividend. Now on its sixth wave of data collection, the Study collected critical information on fertility, education, employment, mental health, and gender-based violence. These findings were presented in national and local fora, most importantly during the Senate and House Committee hearings on the proposed Adolescent Pregnancy Prevention bill. Policy briefs on early sexual initiation and internet use among surveyed children were published for policymakers&#x2019; consumption. Study findings were also provided to nine national government agencies, four local government units, five CSOs, and nine development partners. A UNFPA event during the International Day of the Girl Child attracted significant media attention, and study findings were featured prominently in leading broadsheets.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported UNFPA to continue its 15-year Longitudinal Cohort Study on the Filipino Child. The Cohort Study tracks the lives of 5,000 children to generate evidence for policymakers to address the developmental needs of Filipino youth and harness the country&#x2019;s demographic dividend. Now into its eighth year, the Study has collected critical data on fertility, education, employment, mental health, and gender-based violence. This year, UNFPA signed a memorandum of understanding with the Second Congressional Commission on Education, ensuring that Cohort Study findings will inform recommendations to improve the country&#x2019;s basic education. This was the first official partnership between UNFPA and the Philippine Government, using the Study&#x2019;s findings to deliver policy reform. The Study&#x2019;s findings also informed the 4th National Plan of Action for Children 2023-2028, the Philippine Government&#x2019;s blueprint on child protection. UNICEF featured the findings on its Situation of the Children platform, the most comprehensive portal for child rights monitoring in the Philippines. To date, findings have been cascaded to 18 government agencies, 15 civil society organisations, and 12 development partners.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
